        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-34763",
        ":question/problem": "If $D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,$ then $\\text{rank}(D)=$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "To find the rank of $D,$ we first reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $3$ non-zero rows).\n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(D)=3.$"
